 Bovine Shuffle — analysis · USACO official analysis
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  (Analysis by Nick Wu)

  Treat the shuffle as a functional graph: position i has one edge to aᵢ. A position stays
  occupied forever exactly when it lies on a cycle, since every other position eventually empties.
  Repeatedly delete positions with in-degree 0 (a queue, as in topological sort); what remains are
  the cycles. This is O(N).

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) if (indeg[i] == 0) q.push(i);
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int v = q.front(); q.pop(); alive[v] = false;
        if (--indeg[a[v]] == 0) q.push(a[v]);
    }
























